<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Every package version used by this repository is declared here, once. Projects
    write <PackageReference Include="..." /> with no Version attribute.

    JustDummies is the one that matters. The playground demonstrates the library a
    visitor is about to install, so it must reference a *published* version, never
    a source build: a playground running main would offer constraints that exist
    in no package, and the visitor would find them missing minutes later. This
    repository is deliberately separate from the library's precisely so that a
    project reference is not even possible.

    The version below is a pre-release. That is not an oversight: every
    JustDummies package is one as of this commit, which is also why the site's
    install command needs the prerelease flag until a stable version ships.
    Raising the version here is the whole of the upgrade, because the interface
    reads the version it actually loaded rather than a string typed beside it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="JustDummies" Version="1.0.0-preview.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.DevServer" Version="10.0.10" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The story the site tells is a test being written, so the snippets it publishes have
    to *be* tests: [Fact], Assert, and nothing else. §5.6 asks for the most ordinary
    runner and assertions available, on the grounds that a visitor meeting constrained
    dummies, the Any. syntax and an unfamiliar assertion library at the same time has
    three focal points and therefore none.

    Referenced by the snippet project alone, which compiles what the site displays and
    ships nothing. The alternative — declaring a local FactAttribute so the code
    compiles without the dependency — would put names on the page that resolve to
    something other than what a reader would get, which is the exact class of error
    that project exists to prevent.

    extensibility.core rather than core: the latter's targets refuse to build anything
    that is not an executable test project, and say so in as many words. The snippet
    project is a library that is compiled and never run, so it takes the pair xUnit
    itself points at — the attributes and the assertions, without the runner.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="JustDummies.Xunit" Version="1.0.0-preview.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="3.2.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3.assert" Version="3.2.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3.extensibility.core" Version="3.2.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
